 absence of a myoepithelial layer around small infiltrating ducts  supporting the diagnosis of invasive carcinoma  Deeper level sections of block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  were subsequently performed and demonstrate the invasive and in situ carcinoma to better advantage  The resection margins of the specimen were inked and microscopically evaluated  By his her signature below  the pathologist listed as making the Final Diagnosis certifies that he she has personally reviewed this case and confirmed or corrected the diagnoses  GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME   REMOVE_MRN and  right breast lumpectomy   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2 3cm excision specimen containing 2 needle localization wires  and oriented by the surgeon as follows  Short stitch is anterior  long stitch is lateral  bowtie is cephalad  The long axis of the specimen is the medial to lateral axis and the specimen is inked as follows  Anterior blue  posterior black  superior green  inferior yellow  The specimen is serially sectioned to reveal a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 2cm yellow nodule surrounded by fibrous tissue  next to a metal clip  consistent with prior biopsy site  located 0 9cm from the inferior margin  1 7cm from the superior margin  0 9cm from the posterior margin  1 8cm from the anterior margin  0 8cm from the lateral margin  and 2 0cm from the medial margin  The specimen is submitted as follows   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Entirely submitted prior biopsy site  with the area where the metal clip was found in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Fibrous tissue  lateral to biopsy site  from medial to lateral   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Fibrous tissue  medial to biopsy site  from lateral to medial   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Medial margin  en face   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Lateral margin  en face  Final Diagnosis by Ronald Balassanian M D   Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE ADDENDUM NOTE  REASON  1 QUANTITATIVE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR RECEPTOR PROTEINS Breast  Right  block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    The tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein  The tumor cells stain for progesterone receptor protein  The tumor cells do not show overexpression of HER 2 c erbB 2  See note  Note  Estrogen and progesterone receptor quantitation  The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the tumor cells is assessed visually and reported as follows  The tumor cells stain    10  of nuclei stained  Some tumor cells stain  1 to  10  of nuclei stained  Rare tumor cells stain    1  of nuclei stained  The tumor cells do not stain  0  of nuclei stained  HER 2 c erbB 2 quantitation  the tumor cells membrane immunoreactivity is evaluated visually and reported as follows  Do not show overexpression  0 to 1   no minimal immunoreactivity or incomplete membrane staining of tumor cells  Show moderate  2   overexpression  weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in   10  of tumor cells  Show strong  3   overexpression  strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in   10  of tumor cells  These tests were developed and their performance characteristics determined by the Pathology Department at the Massachusetts General Hospital  They have not been cleared or approved by the U S  Food and Drug Administration  FDA   The FDA has determined that such clearance or approval is not necessary  this laboratory has established and verified the test s accuracy and precision  Addendum  1 by Atul K Bhan M D   Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE ADDENDUM NOTE  REASON  2 RESULTS OF FLUORESCENCE IN SITU HYBRIDIZATION  FISH  FOR HER 2 NEU GENE AMPLIFICATION Breast  Right  block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    FISH analysis failed on the specimen despite repeated attempts  See note  Note  Results are expressed as the average ratio of HER 2 signals to chromosome 17 signals in 60 tumor cell nuclei  A ratio of greater than REMOVED_DATE or equal to REMOVED_DATE indicates  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  neu gene amplification adjusted for chromosome 17 polys